rial\nobjects randomly, result in rapid mixing of the corresponding Markov chain.\nAlthough Conjecture 1.1 is still open, there is a series of results that prove the\nrapid mixing of the swap Markov chain on various special degree sequence classes.\nWe summarize these results in a very compact way in Table 1 without presenting\nthe sometimes lengthy deﬁnitions of the special classes, which can be found in the\nreferences provided. Most rapid mixing results on directed degree sequences can be\nreduced to the case of bipartite degree sequences, as shown in [7]. Since this is also\n2--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConjectured lower bound for 0 ≤ α ≤ +∞ (https://www.maths.usyd.edu.au/u/Asia-Pacific-APDESeminar/slides/Derek_Kielty_talk_AUgust30_2021.pdf)\nciteturn5search14 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 7 months ago; Conjecture (Robin Gap Conjecture; Andrews, Clutterbuck, Hauer)\nConjectured lower bound for 0 ≤ α ≤ +∞\n\nI = interval of length Diam(D)\n\nConjecture (Robin Gap Conjecture; Andrews, Clutterbuck, Hauer)\n\nLet n ≥ 2 and 0 ≤ α ≤ ∞. If D ⊂ ℝⁿ is a convex domain then\n\n(λ₂ − λ₁)(D, α) ≥ (λ₂ − λ₁)(I, α).\n\nHolds for rectangular boxes and equality is attained in the limit of rectangular boxes collapsing to a line segment (Laugesen 2019).\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nTwo small plots are shown near the bottom center. The left plot is titled “Robin Gap of trapezoid” and shows two increasing curves, a blue upper curve and a red lower curve, with a legend in the lower right. The right plot shows a trapezoid diagram with labeled points/segments and a red “P1” label inside.\n</visual_element>\n\nFooter: Derek Kielty — Degeneration of Robin gap — August 30, 2021 — 5 / 16--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe fundamental gap conjecture (https://tu-dresden.de/mn/math/analysis/ressourcen/dateien/osem/2022-06-16_OS_ANA_Fachvortrag_1_Habilverf_Hauer_Abstract.pdf)\nciteturn5searc